    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Biomechanical forces are critical to our understanding of physiological and pathological phenomena. However, current methods of quantifying biophysical forces, such as traction force microscopy, colonic stents, and more are often limited in scope and/or reliability. To remedy this, we need less invasive, biocompatible force sensors with high spatial resolution and stable readout signal that changes with applied force. \r\n\r\nIn this thesis, we address this issue by designing probes based on upconverting nanoparticles (UCNPs) embedded in polymer matrices for use as biophysical force sensors. Using an AFM-confocal microscope assembly, we characterize the emissions of five spin-coated thin film samples of UCNP-polymer probes, varying Mn2+ dopant concentration, UCNP core-shell structure, and host polymer type. We replicate the linear increase in the percent change of the integrated red:green ratio of UCNP emitted light with increasing Mn2+ concentration seen in previous studies with UCNPs embedded in PDMS and alginate gels. We additionally identify that Mn2+ co-doping increases UCNP-PDMS mechanosensitivity up to a certain limit within 4.2% Mn concentration. Finally, we find that Mn2+ as a crosslinking agent in a less rigid alginate matrix similarly increases the colorimetric optical response of core-double-shell UCNPs, suggesting that UCNP structures with dopants moved to shell layers or the external polymer host are still effective. By demonstrating our ability to adapt and optimize force-sensitive UCNPs in different polymer hosts, we take preliminary steps toward implementing them in target biological systems to develop them further as biophysical force probes.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
        {
